# RiftRace Database Rules

## Database
Use PostgreSQL.

Use Flyway for schema evolution.

Every schema change must be represented by a migration committed to Git.

Never rely on manually editing production tables.

## Core concepts
The data model should distinguish:
- application user;
- Riot account;
- challenge;
- challenge participant;
- duo;
- match;
- match participant;
- rank/LP snapshots or equivalent historical data;
- challenge refresh state.

An application user owns challenges.
A Riot account represents a League account independently from application authentication.

Do not assume one application user equals one Riot account unless the product explicitly requires it.

## Challenge
A challenge contains at least:
- owner;
- name;
- type (SOLOQ or DUOQ);
- start timestamp;
- creation/update timestamps;
- lifecycle/status information as needed.

Challenge start time must be stored as an unambiguous timestamp.

## Participants
SOLOQ:
- maximum 16 participants.

DUOQ:
- maximum 8 duos;
- maximum 16 players;
- each duo contains exactly 2 players.

Enforce important limits in backend business logic and database constraints where practical.

## Riot match data
Avoid storing the entire Riot API response blindly unless required.

Persist normalized data needed for:
- participant identification;
- match deduplication;
- win/loss;
- queue/type;
- timestamps;
- LP/rank progression;
- DuoQ detection.

Use Riot match IDs as a natural deduplication key where appropriate.

## Refresh tracking
Persist enough information to enforce the challenge refresh policy.

At minimum, the system must be able to determine the last accepted refresh for a challenge and calculate the next permitted refresh time.

The frontend must never be the source of truth for this.

## Indexing
Consider indexes for:
- challenge ID;
- owner ID;
- challenge participant ID;
- Riot PUUID;
- Riot match ID;
- match timestamp;
- challenge refresh lookup.

Do not add indexes blindly; justify expensive indexes based on access patterns.

## Data integrity
Use:
- foreign keys;
- unique constraints;
- not-null constraints where appropriate;
- sensible cascading rules.

Avoid orphaned challenge participants, duos or match records.
